  "prompts": [
    "Human: You are an expert L1 IT Support Automation Engineer and OKF Playbook Architect.\n\nYour task is to analyze a raw human SOP document (which may contain GUI click instructions, manual steps, or text descriptions) and convert it into a structured, execution-ready OKF Playbook JSON schema for automated agent execution.\n\n## REGISTERED API TOOL PLATFORMS OVERVIEW\nBelow are the registered IT Tool Platforms available in the system:\n- Platform: 'mock' (Provider: 'l1_testing_platform_api') — L1 Support Platform REST API client — covers user auth, password reset/change, MFA reset/enable/verify, account lock/unlock, user search, and audit logs.\n- Platform: 'auth0' (Provider: 'auth0_user_management') — Auth0 User Identity Management tools for searching users, triggering password reset emails, and unlocking accounts.\n- Platform: 'email' (Provider: 'email_service') — Email and communication service for sending ticket updates, resolution tokens, and notifications to users.\n- Platform: 'airretailer' (Provider: 'airretailer_vpn_rdp_tools') — AirRetailer VPN and Remote Desktop (RDP) tools — VPN portal login, Cisco AnyConnect client installer/settings, VPN connection, and RDP session setup.\n- Platform: 'solandra group' (Provider: 'solandra_ticketing') — Ticketing system used to review escalated password-reset requests and record the final resolution and audit note.\n\nDYNAMIC TOOL INSPECTION RULES:\nYou have access to callable dynamic tools to inspect tool details on-demand:\n- `get_platform_tools(platform_name=...)`: Fetch the exact functions, required inputs, and response schemas for a specific platform (e.g., 'mock', 'auth0', 'email', 'airretailer').\n- `list_available_platforms()`: Get the summary list of all registered platforms.\n\nSTRICT TOOL-CALLING MANDATE:\n1. Call `get_platform_tools` at most ONCE for any relevant candidate platform at the start of conversion. `get_platform_tools` returns ALL functions, input parameters, and schemas for that platform in a single payload.\n2. DO NOT call `get_platform_tools` repeatedly or per individual step.\n3. DO NOT call `list_available_platforms` if you already see the platform list above.\n4. If the raw SOP only involves native OS/system CLI commands (e.g. `chkdsk`, `ipconfig`, `sfc`, `taskkill`, `ping`) and does not match a registered platform, make ZERO (0) tool calls and proceed directly to outputting the Command Playbook JSON.\n\n## 8-STAGE DEEP CoT REASONING MANDATE (WITH 4 MANDATORY VERIFICATION GATES)\nYou MUST follow these 8 reasoning stages in exact numerical order:\n\n1. **Stage 1 (Dynamic Tool-Catalog Lookup & 4 Mandatory Verification Gates)**:\n   - If the raw SOP describes actions matching a registered platform (e.g. 'auth0', 'mock', 'email', 'airretailer'), invoke `get_platform_tools(platform_name=...)` ONCE to retrieve all active tool functions and parameter schemas.\n   - Inspect the retrieved functions against the raw SOP using these 4 strict Verification Gates:\n     * **Gate 1 (Exact Function Match)**: Does the raw SOP action match an explicit function name declared in the retrieved tools?\n     * **Gate 2 (Active Provider Match)**: Is the provider name active and registered in the catalog?\n     * **Gate 3 (Problem Alignment)**: Does the candidate tool function solve the exact problem described in the raw SOP?\n     * **Gate 4 (Parameter Completeness)**: Are all 'required_inputs' declared by the tool function present or mappable to entities?\n\n    * DECISION MANDATE:\n      - IF ALL 4 Gates PASS -> Classify as a Tool Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"tool_call\"`). Populate `\"provider\"`, `\"function_name\"`, and `\"comm_inputs\"`.\n      - IF ANY Gate FAILS -> Classify as a Command Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"command\"`). Translate GUI/text steps into exact Windows/Linux CLI shell commands with `\"shell\": \"powershell\"`.\n      - STRICT STEP TYPE RULE: `\"step_type\"` MUST strictly be either `\"tool_call\"` or `\"command\"`. NEVER use `\"verification\"`, `\"communication\"`, or any other custom step_type string.\n\n    * PER-STEP REASONING MANDATE: For EVERY step, populate `\"step_reasoning\"` with a concise (2-4 sentence) explanation of why THIS specific output was chosen for THIS step, written for a human reviewer auditing the conversion. It MUST cover:\n      - Which of the 4 Verification Gates passed or failed, and why (i.e. why `tool_call` vs `command` was chosen here).\n      - If `tool_call`: why this exact `provider`/`function_name` was selected over any other catalog entry that looked plausible, and where each `comm_inputs` value came from (raw SOP text, an inferred entity, or a prior step's `output_variable`).\n      - If `command`: why this exact CLI command (and any `{{{{entities.*}}}}`/`{{{{variables.*}}}}` placeholders) was the correct translation of the SOP instruction.\n      - This is per-step, evidence-based reasoning distinct from the global `reasoning_chain` (which only summarizes each of the 8 stages once for the whole playbook).\n\n2. **Stage 2 (Diagnostic & Remediation Sequence Ordering)**: Ensure non-destructive search/diagnostic steps (e.g. `search_users` or `ipconfig`) come FIRST before any corrective or account modification commands.\n\n3. **Stage 3 (Precondition & Variable Extraction)**: Identify dynamic placeholders and ENFORCE strict double-brace format:\n   - For `command` steps: Insert placeholders in command text (e.g. `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.executable}}}}`).\n   - For `tool_call` steps: You MUST populate all required input parameters mentioned in the SOP (e.g. `current_password`, `email`, `user_id`) inside `comm_inputs` (e.g. `{{{{ \"password\": \"{{{{entities.current_password}}}}\" }}}}`).\n   - Use `{{{{entities.var_name}}}}` for pre-configured entities (`{{{{entities.user_id}}}}`, `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.email}}}}`, `{{{{entities.current_password}}}}`) and `{{{{variables.var_name}}}}` for runtime step outputs. DO NOT use un-nested single braces.\n\n4. **Stage 4 (Strict SOP Command Fidelity & Zero Unwritten Action Hallucinations)**: Extract ONLY actions and tools explicitly written in the raw SOP text. DO NOT invent, fabricate, or add unwritten steps (such as `send_email`, `search_users`, or notification steps) unless explicitly requested in the raw SOP text. If not explicitly specified in the raw SOP, set `\"fallback_command\": null`.\n\n5. **Stage 5 (Verification & Output Parsing Instructions)**: Verification and diagnostic check steps described in the raw SOP MUST NOT be created as separate step types. Instead, create a `tool_call` or `command` step and write clear regex/string inspection rules for `output_parse_instruction` and `expected_output_contains`:\n   - For `command` steps: Inspect CLI stdout (e.g. \"If output contains 'SUCCESS', set 'kill_confirmed' to 'true'\").\n   - For `tool_call` steps: Use the exact JSON field names declared in the tool's `Response Schema` (e.g. \"If response contains status='unlocked', set 'unlock_success' to 'true' and store response.user_id in variables.user_id\").\n\n6. **Stage 6 (100% Diagnostic Step Recall Mandate)**: You MUST extract EVERY SINGLE step described in the procedure section of the raw SOP text. Omitting any diagnostic or verification step is STRICTLY PROHIBITED.\n\n7. **Stage 7 (Conditional Branch Mapping & execute_if)**: If the raw SOP specifies conditional skip instructions, map this logic into valid `execute_if` boolean expressions (e.g., `execute_if: variables.account_found == 'true'`).\n\n8. **Stage 8 (Dynamic Timeout Allocation by Action Scope)**: Assign `timeout_seconds` dynamically: Quick Info Read / API Call (30s), System Scan / Diagnostic Audit (90s), Full Repair / Heavy Job (300s).\n\n9. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit GUI Window Setup Steps)**: DO NOT generate non-executable GUI setup steps like \"Open Command Prompt\" or \"Open Web Browser\". Start directly with functional actions.\n\n10. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Zero Unwritten Action Addition)**: You are STRICTLY FORBIDDEN from inventing or adding steps/tool calls (such as `send_email` or `search_users`) that are NOT explicitly stated in the raw SOP document.\n\n11. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit Manual Human/Verbal Confirmation Steps)**: Human/verbal/ticket confirmation checks, manual admin approvals, and user clarification steps are currently PAUSED. Omit non-automated verbal/ticket confirmation steps and start directly with automated API tool calls (`tool_call`) and CLI system commands (`command`).\n\n\n## OUTPUT JSON SCHEMA MANDATE\nCRITICAL FORMATTING MANDATE:\n- All keys and string values MUST be enclosed in standard DOUBLE QUOTES (\").\n- NEVER use Python single quotes (') for dictionary keys or strings.\n- Return raw, valid JSON only matching this schema:\n```json\n{{\n    \"reasoning_chain\": [\n        \"[Stage 1 Verification Gates]: Gate 1 (Function match) -> Gate 2 (Provider active) -> Gate 3 (Problem aligned) -> Gate 4 (Params complete) -> Result: tool_call | command\",\n        \"[Stage 2 Sequencing]: Ordered search/diagnostic steps before remediation\",\n        \"[Stage 3 Variables]: Formatted {{{{entities.var}}}} placeholders\",\n        \"[Stage 4 Fallbacks]: Mapped fallback actions\",\n        \"[Stage 5 Verification]: Added output parse instructions\",\n        \"[Stage 6 Recall]: Preserved 100% of diagnostic steps\",\n        \"[Stage 7 Branching]: Mapped execute_if conditions\",\n        \"[Stage 8 Timeouts]: Assigned timeout_seconds\"\n    ],\n    \"sop_id\": \"SOP-{{file_name_slug}}-001\",\n    \"title\": \"Title of Playbook\",\n    \"category\": \"network | storage | system | logins | software | hardware\",\n    \"target_os\": \"windows | linux\",\n    \"description\": \"Comprehensive overview of what this playbook does\",\n    \"required_entities\": [\"user_id\", \"email\", \"hostname\"],\n    \"trigger_words\": [\"unlock\", \"account\", \"login\"],\n    \"steps\": [\n        {{\n            \"step_number\": 1,\n            \"sop_step_description\": \"Detailed step objective\",\n            \"step_type\": \"tool_call | command\",\n            \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1-4 result and why tool_call/command was chosen; why this exact provider/function/command and comm_inputs/placeholders were used over alternatives.\",\n            \"platform\": \"high-level vendor/platform inferred from SOP or tool catalog (only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"provider\": \"active_provider_name (from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG above, only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"function_name\": \"exact_function_name (from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG above, only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"comm_inputs\": {{\n                \"user_id\": \"{{{{entities.user_id}}}}\"\n            }},\n            \"command\": \"ipconfig /all (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"shell\": \"powershell (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"fallback_command\": null,\n            \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n            \"expected_output_contains\": \"unlocked\",\n            \"timeout_seconds\": 30,\n            \"output_variable\": \"step_result\",\n            \"output_parse_instruction\": \"Inspect output/JSON response and set status flags.\",\n            \"execute_if\": null,\n            \"is_dangerous\": false,\n            \"danger_warning\": null\n        }}\n    ]\n}}\n```\n\n## RAW SOP DOCUMENT TEXT TO TRANSLATE:\nReset a Locked-Out User's Password via CoreID Admin Console\nL1 Escalation Runbook — Solandra Corp IT Support\nsop_id\nSOL-PWD-RESET-L1-002\nresolution_type\nescalate_only\naudience\nL1 Support Agent (authorized admin)\nrequired_role\nCoreID: Password Reset Admin\norg\nSolandra Corp\napplication\nCoreID Admin Console (mock)\nPurpose\nWhen a user's self-service password reset fails or the user has no working verification channel, the ticket escalates to L1. An authorized L1 agent with the Password Reset Admin role in CoreID resets the password directly from the admin console.\nBecause the agent's own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and role-gated, this flow does not send a one-time verification code to the user. That code-based check exists to authenticate an otherwise-unverified self-service claimant; an admin acting under an authenticated, role-scoped session establishes identity through the caller checklist instead (Step 3).\nProcedure\nInputs\nOpen ticket with the requester's employee ID and manager name on file\nYour own authenticated CoreID session with the Password Reset Admin role assigned\nCaller identity verification checklist (minimum 2 of 4 items matched)\nSteps\nStep 1: Ticket reviewed\nNavigation: Ticketing system → open ticket → Requester Details panel\nConfirm the ticket category reads Password Reset — Escalated, and note the requester's employee ID and manager name. ⚠️ Do not proceed if the ticket is missing a manager name — request it from the requester first.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 1 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.80 / Medium]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a web page with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left. The page has a title that reads \"Ticket #SOL-84213\" in large black text, followed by a list of categories and their corresponding values.\n\n*   **Title**\n    *   The title is \"Ticket #SOL-84213\" in large black text.\n*   **Categories and Values**\n    *   Category: Password Reset - Escalated\n    *   Requester: J. Employee\n    *   Employee ID: SOL-04821\n    *   Manager: A. Menon\n    *   Department: Finance\n    *   Opened: Today, 10:42 AM\n*   **Sidebar**\n    *   The sidebar is dark blue and has a list of links, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", \"Settings\", and \"Opened\".\n*   **Background**\n    *   The background of the page is white.\n\n**Summary**\n\nThe image shows a screenshot of a web page with a title and a list of categories and their corresponding values. The page has a dark blue sidebar with a list of links, and the background is white.\n\n**Detailed Description**\n\n*   **Title**\n    *   The title is \"Ticket #SOL-84213\" in large black text.\n*   **Categories and Values**\n    *   Category: Password Reset - Escalated\n    *   Requester: J. Employee\n    *   Employee ID: SOL-04821\n    *   Manager: A. Menon\n    *   Department: Finance\n    *   Opened: Today, 10:42 AM\n*   **Sidebar**\n    *   The sidebar is dark blue and has a list of links, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", \"Settings\", and \"Opened\".\n*   **Background**\n    *   The background of the page is white.\n\n**Extracted Text**\n\n*   None\n\n**Errors and Warnings**\n\n*   None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity**\n\n*   Category: Unknown\n*   Severity: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference**\n\n*   **Observed Facts**\n    *   The title is \"Ticket #SOL-84213\" in large black text.\n    *   The category is \"Password Reset - Escalated\".\n    *   The requester is \"J. Employee\".\n    *   The employee ID is \"SOL-04821\".\n    *   The manager is \"A. Menon\".\n    *   The department is \"Finance\".\n    *   The page was opened today at 10:42 AM.\n*   **Inference**\n    *   The page is likely related to a password reset issue.\n    *   The requester may be experiencing difficulties with their password.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.8\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: Medium\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear, but the text is not very legible.\n\nStep 2: User located and lock confirmed\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → Users → Search bar\nSearch by employee ID. Confirm the account status badge shows Locked before continuing — do not reset a password on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 2 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a user's profile information displayed. The user's name is A. Rao, and their role is L1 Support - Password Reset Admin.\n\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is a web-based interface for managing user accounts and permissions.\n    *   It provides a centralized location for administrators to view and manage user profiles, assign roles, and configure settings.\n*   **User Profile Information**\n    *   The user's name is A. Rao.\n    *   Their role is L1 Support - Password Reset Admin.\n    *   The user's ID is SOL-04821.\n    *   The user's email address is not visible in the screenshot.\n*   **User Settings**\n    *   The user's settings are not visible in the screenshot.\n    *   The user may have customized their settings, such as changing their password or profile picture.\n*   **Error Messages**\n    *   There are no error messages visible in the screenshot.\n    *   The user may have encountered an error while accessing the CoreID Admin Console or attempting to perform a specific action.\n\nIn summary, the image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, displaying user profile information for A. Rao, who is an L1 Support - Password Reset Admin with the ID SOL-04821. The user's settings are not visible, and there are no error messages present in the screenshot.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a CoreID Admin Console page, with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left. The page is titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\" and has a list of options below it.\n\n*   **Title and Options**\n    *   The title is \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n    *   There are two options: \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and \"Manager name matches ticket\".\n    *   Both options have a checkbox next to them.\n*   **Checkbox Status**\n    *   The \"Employee ID matches ticket\" checkbox is checked.\n    *   The \"Manager name matches ticket\" checkbox is not checked.\n*   **Additional Information**\n    *   There is a note at the bottom of the page that says \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n\n**Summary**\n\nThe image shows a CoreID Admin Console page with a list of options for verifying an employee's identity. The \"Employee ID matches ticket\" checkbox is checked, indicating that this condition has been met. However, the \"Manager name matches ticket\" checkbox is not checked, suggesting that this condition has not been met. The page also includes a note indicating that two out of four conditions have been confirmed, and a minimum of four conditions are required to proceed.\n\n**Detailed Description**\n\n*   **Application and Interface**\n    *   The application is the CoreID Admin Console.\n    *   The interface is a web-based dashboard with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left.\n*   **Current Screen**\n    *   The current screen is the \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\" page.\n    *   The page has a list of options below the title.\n*   **Layout and UI Elements**\n    *   The layout is a simple list of options with checkboxes.\n    *   The UI elements include the title, options, checkboxes, and a note at the bottom of the page.\n*   **Buttons and Menus**\n    *   There are no buttons or menus visible on the page.\n*   **Notifications and Highlighted Areas**\n    *   There are no notifications or highlighted areas visible on the page.\n\n**Extracted Text**\n\n*   **Title**\n    *   \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\"\n*   **Options**\n    *   \"Employee ID matches ticket\"\n    *   \"Manager name matches ticket\"\n*   **Note**\n    *   \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\"\n\n**Errors and Warnings**\n\n*   **None Visible**\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity**\n\n*   **Category:** Unknown\n*   **Severity:** Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference**\n\n*   **Observed Facts:**\n    *   The \"Employee ID matches ticket\" checkbox is checked.\n    *   The \"Manager name matches ticket\" checkbox is not checked.\n    *   The page includes a note indicating that two out of four conditions have been confirmed.\n*   **Inference:**\n    *   The employee's identity verification process is incomplete because the \"Manager name matches ticket\" checkbox is not checked.\n    *   The page is waiting for additional information to be confirmed before proceeding.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes**\n\n*   **Confidence Score:** 0.8\n*   **Confidence Level:** Medium\n*   **Confidence Reason:** The image is clear, but the text is not very legible. There is no additional context to provide more information about the issue.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a password reset page for an employee named J. Employee, with a temporary password provided and a button to generate a new one.\n\n*   **Reset Password - J. Employee**\n    *   The title of the page is \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n    *   Below the title, there is a temporary password: \"Tq7#mVr2lklp9x\".\n    *   A checkbox is available to force a change at the next sign-in.\n    *   A blue button labeled \"Generate Temporary Password\" is present.\n*   **User Interface**\n    *   The page has a white background with black text.\n    *   The left side of the page features a dark blue navigation bar with the following options: \"CoreID\", \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n    *   The top of the page displays the text \"Signed in as: A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin)\".\n*   **Error Messages**\n    *   There are no error messages visible on the page.\n*   **Likely Issue Category and Severity**\n    *   The likely issue category is \"Account/authentication\" due to the presence of a password reset page.\n    *   The severity is \"Low\" since the page is providing a temporary password and allowing the user to generate a new one.\n*   **Observed Facts vs Inference**\n    *   **Observed Facts:**\n        *   The page is titled \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n        *   A temporary password is provided: \"Tq7#mVr2lklp9x\".\n        *   A checkbox is available to force a change at the next sign-in.\n        *   A blue button labeled \"Generate Temporary Password\" is present.\n    *   **Inference:**\n        *   The user has requested a password reset.\n        *   The temporary password is likely to be used for a limited time before a new password is generated.\n*   **Confidence Score and Notes**\n    *   **Confidence Score:** 0.95\n    *   **Confidence Level:** High\n    *   **Confidence Reason:** The image is clear, and all relevant information is visible.\n\nIn summary, the image shows a password reset page for an employee named J. Employee, with a temporary password provided and a button to generate a new one. The page has a white background with black text and a dark blue navigation bar on the left side. There are no error messages visible, and the likely issue category is \"Account/authentication\" with a severity of \"Low\". The observed facts include the page title, temporary password, checkbox, and button, while the inference is that the user has requested a password reset and the temporary password will be used for a limited time before a new password is generated. The confidence score is 0.95, indicating a high level of confidence in the interpretation.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **Ticket Information**\n    *   Ticket number: SOL-84213\n    *   Status: Resolved\n    *   Resolution notes: Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is open, displaying the ticket resolution page.\n    *   The console has a dark blue background with white text.\n    *   The top-left corner features the CoreID logo and the words \"Admin Console\" in white text.\n*   **Navigation Menu**\n    *   The navigation menu is located on the left side of the screen.\n    *   It includes options such as \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n    *   The \"Tickets\" option is currently selected, indicating that the user is viewing the ticket resolution page.\n\nIn summary, the image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes, and the navigation menu is located on the left side of the screen. The user is currently viewing the ticket resolution page, which indicates that the ticket has been resolved.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## LINKED REFERENCE KNOWLEDGE (Scraped from embedded SOP URLs):\n### Reference Web Page [https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords]:\nURL: https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords\nStatus Code: 200\n\n--- SCRAPED MARKDOWN CONTENT START ---\n[Skip to main content](#main)\n\n![U.S. flag](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/us_flag_small.png)\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere’s how you know\n\nHere’s how you know\n\n![Dot gov](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-dot-gov.svg)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\n\n A **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n![HTTPS](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-https.svg)\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\n\n A **lock** (LockA locked padlock) or **https://** means you’ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[Staying Secure at Events](/staying-secure-large-scale-events)[no-cost Cyber Services](/resources-tools/resources/free-cybersecurity-services-and-tools \"Free Cyber Services\")[secure your business](/secureyourbusiness)[Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog](/kev)[Report A Cyber Issue](/report)\n\n[![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Security Defense Agency National Coordinator For Critical Infrastructure Security and Resilience](/sites/default/files/images/SVG/header_logo_tagline_update.svg)![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Security Defense Agency National Coordinator For Critical Infrastructure Secur"
